# IMPORTANT NOTE (Ownership)

### ❌ Wrong

```
Pizza* p = new PlainPizza();

Cheese* c = new Cheese(p);

Olives* o = new Olives(p);
```

Object graph:

```
         PlainPizza
         /        \
    Cheese      Olives
```

Both decorators own the same object.

Both destructors execute:

```
delete p;
```

Result:

❌ Double deletion

❌ Undefined Behavior

---

### ✅ Correct

```
Pizza* p = new PlainPizza();

p = new Cheese(p);

p = new Olives(p);
```

Object graph:

```
Olives
   │
Cheese
   │
PlainPizza
```

Each decorator owns exactly **one** object.

Deletion chain:

```
delete Olives
      │
delete Cheese
      │
delete PlainPizza
```

Everything is deleted exactly once.

---

# Advantages

* Dynamically adds functionality.
* Avoids class explosion.
* Follows Open/Closed Principle.
* Flexible and reusable.
* Uses composition over inheritance.

---

# Disadvantages

* More objects are created.
* Debugging can be harder.
* Long decorator chains may become difficult to understand.

---

# Real-World Examples

* File Compression + Encryption + Logging
* Network Socket + SSL + Compression
* Java I/O Streams
* GUI Widgets (Border, Scrollbar, Shadow)
* HTTP Middleware
* Text Formatting

---

# Decorator vs Adapter

| Decorator             | Adapter                           |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Adds new behavior     | Converts one interface to another |
| Same interface        | Different interface               |
| Wraps object          | Wraps object                      |
| Extends functionality | Improves compatibility            |

---

# Interview One-Liner

**Decorator is used when an object has multiple optional features that can be combined in different ways. 
Instead of creating a subclass for every feature combination, we create one decorator per feature and compose them dynamically at runtime.**
